{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And when an ox gores.</b> This passage is mentioned because of whether the ox gores a slave or a maidservant, and this man and the maidservant are Israelites. And he mentioned the woman but did not do so in the verse \"one who strikes a man and he dies\" (Exodus 21:12), for it is the way of language to mention the honored one, who is the male, and the same applies to the female. And he mentioned the woman now, because there are places where it is not the custom of a woman to go outside the city to the field, to the place where the oxen are, lest a person think and object and say, why did the custom change. And because of this it is written after it \"whether it gores a son or a daughter\" (Exodus 21:31), for the law of one who strikes a man is the same whether he be older in years or younger, and he mentioned the son and the daughter, who are young, lest the owner of the ox object against the fathers, that it was upon them to guard them:" "<b>The ox shall surely be stoned.</b> The reason of <b>and its flesh shall not be eaten</b> is like carrion, which a resident alien or a foreigner may eat. And our predecessors said that every place where it is written \"shall not be eaten\" is forbidden for benefit:" "<b>And the owner of the ox is clear.</b> From punishment."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:10.181284314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}